---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Navbar from "../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

// Keep in step with version.json in this repo's root — that is what the in-app
// update check reads, and a page pointing at an older release than the manifest
// hands new users a build the app immediately wants to replace.
const version = "0.9.1";
const dmgURL = `https://github.com/Pratik-Singh-web/minnie-landing/releases/download/v${version}-beta/Minnie-${version}-beta.dmg`;
const expires = "7 November 2026";

const h2 = "prose-h2";
const p = "prose-p";
const ul = "prose-ul";
const link = "prose-a";
---

<Layout
  title="Download the Minnie beta — free"
  description="Download the free Minnie beta for Apple Silicon Macs. Minnie is a native macOS desktop pet that runs the AI coding agent you already use."
>
  <Navbar />

  <main class="legal">
    <a href={base} class="legal__back mono">← back to home</a>

    <h1 class="legal__h1">Download the beta</h1>
    <p class="legal__date mono">v{version} · free · expires {expires}</p>

    <p class={p} style="margin-top:28px;">
      Minnie is in open beta and free to use. She runs the AI coding agent you already have on
      your Mac, so there's no extra AI bill — but you do need your own agent.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:28px 0 12px;">
      <a href={dmgURL} class="btn">Download Minnie {version} →</a>
    </p>
    <p class="mono" style="opacity:.7;font-size:14px;">
      Apple Silicon · macOS 14+ · ~146 MB
    </p>

    <!-- The install block. Do NOT promise a dialog-free install: the app is
         unsigned, and macOS warns about unsigned apps in ways that vary by
         machine and OS version. Telling someone they'll see no warning and then
         showing them one reads as "this app is broken" — which is the exact
         impression this section exists to prevent. -->
    <h2 class={h2}>Installing</h2>

    <p class={p}>
      Straight up: <strong>macOS will probably warn you about this app</strong>, and you'll
      click past it once. Minnie isn't signed by Apple yet — a Developer ID costs $99/year and
      that comes with v1. Nothing about the warning is specific to Minnie; every unsigned Mac
      app gets it.
    </p>

    <ol class={ul} style="list-style:decimal;">
      <li>Open <code>Minnie-{version}-beta.dmg</code></li>
      <li>
        Paste this into Terminal:
        <pre class="mono" style="overflow-x:auto;padding:14px;margin-top:8px;border:2px solid var(--ink);border-radius:8px;font-size:13px;"><code>ditto --noextattr --norsrc /Volumes/Minnie*/Minnie.app /Applications/Minnie.app
xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Minnie.app
open /Applications/Minnie.app</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        Minnie lives in your <strong>menu bar</strong> (paw icon) — no Dock icon, by design.
        Click it to wake her.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p class={p}>
      <strong>Still blocked?</strong> If you see <em>"Apple could not verify Minnie is free of
      malware"</em>, open <strong>System Settings ▸ Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, scroll to
      the bottom, and click <strong>Open Anyway</strong> next to Minnie. You'll only ever do
      this once.
    </p>

    <p class={p}>
      Use the commands rather than dragging her to Applications: macOS marks the downloaded
      disk image as quarantined and Finder copies that mark onto anything you drag out of it,
      which makes the warning stickier than it needs to be.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>What you need</h2>
    <ul class={ul}>
      <li><strong>An Apple Silicon Mac</strong> (M1 or later). Intel Macs aren't supported yet.</li>
      <li><strong>macOS 14 Sonoma</strong> or later.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your own AI coding agent</strong> — Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Codex, or a custom
        command. Minnie drives the agent you already pay for; she doesn't resell AI.
      </li>
      <li>A microphone, if you want to talk to her. Typing works fine too.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class={h2}>Beta caveats</h2>
    <ul class={ul}>
      <li>Things will break. That's the point — tell me when they do.</li>
      <li>
        Minnie can run real commands on your Mac. She asks before each one; read what you
        approve.
      </li>
      <li>
        This build stops working on <strong>{expires}</strong>. She checks for new versions on
        launch and can update herself, so you shouldn't have to come back here.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class={p} style="margin-top:28px;">
      Something broken, confusing, or delightful?
      <a href="mailto:pratiksingh30701@gmail.com" class={link}>Email me</a> — beta feedback goes
      straight to the person who wrote it.
    </p>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
